00:04:00.170 --> 00:04:01.060
How you doing, Connor?

00:04:01.060 --> 00:04:01.420
CONNOR GILBERT: Pretty well.

00:04:01.420 --> 00:04:02.300
Glad to be here.

00:04:02.300 --> 00:04:02.500
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:04:02.500 --> 00:04:04.150
Well, thank you
so much for coming

00:04:04.150 --> 00:04:06.400
to talk to us about StackRox,
and Kubernetes security,

00:04:06.400 --> 00:04:08.770
and container security, and
all the security things.

00:04:08.770 --> 00:04:09.920
Before we get stuck
in there, do you

00:04:09.920 --> 00:04:11.110
want to tell us a little
bit about yourself

00:04:11.110 --> 00:04:12.260
and your background?

00:04:12.260 --> 00:04:13.135
CONNOR GILBERT: Sure.

00:04:13.135 --> 00:04:16.040
So I'm a product manager on
the product team at StackRox.

00:04:16.040 --> 00:04:18.820
At a small company, that
means dealing with customers,

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:21.237
triaging issues,
understanding their needs,

00:04:21.237 --> 00:04:23.320
developing new features,
figuring out the roadmap,

00:04:23.320 --> 00:04:24.570
then working with engineering.

00:04:24.570 --> 00:04:27.420
So it's really all across the
stack of delivering a quality

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:28.060
product.

00:04:28.060 --> 00:04:28.940
A lot of fun.

00:04:28.940 --> 00:04:29.290
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:04:29.290 --> 00:04:30.915
At a high level, do
you want to tell us

00:04:30.915 --> 00:04:32.200
about what StackRox does?

00:04:32.200 --> 00:04:32.350
CONNOR GILBERT: Yeah.

00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:34.900
So we're a Kubernetes security
company, and what we do

00:04:34.900 --> 00:04:37.750
is try to use the power of
containers and Kubernetes

00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:40.630
to help customers up
their game in security.

00:04:40.630 --> 00:04:43.340
We build a product that
goes across the container

00:04:43.340 --> 00:04:45.730
lifecycle-- so
build, deploy, run--

00:04:45.730 --> 00:04:48.460
and helps them understand
risk, understand

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:51.370
where they might be exposed,
and detect threats and respond

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:52.630
to them in real time.

00:04:52.630 --> 00:04:54.610
GABRIELA FERRARA: What
Google Platform products

00:04:54.610 --> 00:04:56.333
do you use for the
StackRox platform?

00:04:56.333 --> 00:04:58.750
CONNOR GILBERT: Yeah, so we
have an interesting deployment

00:04:58.750 --> 00:04:59.450
model.

00:04:59.450 --> 00:05:01.060
We are a security
product, and we

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:03.130
want to run wherever
customers do, so

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:07.280
across Google Cloud Platform,
other clouds, on-premises.

00:05:07.280 --> 00:05:11.080
So we use a lot of GCP
products inside of our company.

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:13.613
We run a Kubernetes
Engine cluster--

00:05:13.613 --> 00:05:15.530
actually two of them, I
think, on every commit

00:05:15.530 --> 00:05:18.330
into our code base for
testing and validation.

00:05:18.330 --> 00:05:20.517
We use the Google
Container Registry,

00:05:20.517 --> 00:05:22.600
some of the container
analysis products internally

00:05:22.600 --> 00:05:24.860
for deployment and
things like that.

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:26.800
But then when we're
deployed, we act

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:29.380
as a self-contained product.

00:05:29.380 --> 00:05:32.020
We do integrate with the Cloud
Security Command Center, which

00:05:32.020 --> 00:05:34.480
has recently added
Kubernetes Engine cluster

00:05:34.480 --> 00:05:36.600
enumeration and
findings tagging,

00:05:36.600 --> 00:05:38.840
so helping customers
understand their risk there.

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:41.890
But yeah, mostly internal to
our company, we're using GCP

00:05:41.890 --> 00:05:44.410
and really use that to
keep our dev velocity up.

00:05:44.410 --> 00:05:45.160
MARK MANDEL: Nice.

00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:47.090
So actually, I'm curious.

00:05:47.090 --> 00:05:49.960
You said you're a
Kubernetes security company.

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:52.840
What made you decide
that Kubernetes

00:05:52.840 --> 00:05:54.695
was going to be the
platform that you

00:05:54.695 --> 00:05:57.070
were going to base basically
your entire business around?

00:05:57.070 --> 00:05:57.940
CONNOR GILBERT: Yeah,
that's a good question.

00:05:57.940 --> 00:06:00.340
And it was definitely
a choice we made.

00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:02.000
We started it around
four years ago.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:05.500
And around that time, containers
were on the ascendancy,

00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:08.570
but Kubernetes hadn't
really won the game.

00:06:08.570 --> 00:06:10.510
We think really
Kubernetes is the platform

00:06:10.510 --> 00:06:14.780
for portable, robust, repeatable
app management and security.

00:06:14.780 --> 00:06:16.520
So we've seen
widespread adoption.

00:06:16.520 --> 00:06:18.520
We've seen a lot of the
players in the container

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:21.250
space adding support for,
or rearchitecting around,

00:06:21.250 --> 00:06:22.090
Kubernetes.

00:06:22.090 --> 00:06:25.060
And we really think that's
the place where we can focus,

00:06:25.060 --> 00:06:27.190
so we build deep
integrations into Kubernetes.

00:06:27.190 --> 00:06:29.380
We think that gives us
a much better position

00:06:29.380 --> 00:06:32.033
to help our customers do
a better job of security,

00:06:32.033 --> 00:06:33.700
by really using the
power of Kubernetes,

00:06:33.700 --> 00:06:36.715
and not going to a least common
denominator of just containers.

00:06:36.715 --> 00:06:37.673
GABRIELA FERRARA: Yeah.

00:06:37.673 --> 00:06:39.430
And using Kubernetes
on your day-to-day

00:06:39.430 --> 00:06:41.950
and integrating it into
your product, what were

00:06:41.950 --> 00:06:43.928
the challenges that you faced?

00:06:43.928 --> 00:06:45.970
CONNOR GILBERT: So I'll
project a little bit back

00:06:45.970 --> 00:06:47.100
into the past.

00:06:47.100 --> 00:06:50.530
At my last company, we started
using Kubernetes in, I think,

00:06:50.530 --> 00:06:53.870
early 2015, so around the beta
time for Google Kubernetes

00:06:53.870 --> 00:06:54.850
Engine--

00:06:54.850 --> 00:06:57.020
then called, I think,
just Container Engine.

00:06:57.020 --> 00:06:58.960
And there is a bit
of a learning curve.

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:02.120
Now that I've been in this
space for four years or so,

00:07:02.120 --> 00:07:03.830
I like to think I've
got up the curve.

00:07:03.830 --> 00:07:06.370
But we find a lot of customers,
and a lot of just folks

00:07:06.370 --> 00:07:08.890
in the space, community
events, that kind of thing,

00:07:08.890 --> 00:07:10.660
where the knowledge
of Kubernetes,

00:07:10.660 --> 00:07:13.300
the knowledge of containers
hasn't really penetrated

00:07:13.300 --> 00:07:14.890
the entire organization.

00:07:14.890 --> 00:07:16.690
So they've started
with development,

00:07:16.690 --> 00:07:19.040
or maybe the operations
team has started using it.

00:07:19.040 --> 00:07:20.440
Maybe it's in some
stages of the business,

00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:21.357
but not in production.

00:07:21.357 --> 00:07:23.140
But often, security
is left behind.

00:07:23.140 --> 00:07:24.820
So the security
team is often trying

00:07:24.820 --> 00:07:28.300
to catch up and understand,
what the heck is this thing?

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:29.720
What are the consequences?

00:07:29.720 --> 00:07:30.650
How does it deploy?

00:07:30.650 --> 00:07:31.690
How do I monitor it?

00:07:31.690 --> 00:07:34.773
Those are the challenges we see
across a lot of organizations.

00:07:34.773 --> 00:07:36.940
MARK MANDEL: You said
something interesting as well.

00:07:36.940 --> 00:07:38.800
You said, just talking
about StackRox generally,

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:41.050
it sounds like it's both CI
and CD, so just Continuous

00:07:41.050 --> 00:07:42.780
Integration and
Continuous Deployment.

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:46.448
But there is an integrated
security aspect to all of that.

00:07:46.448 --> 00:07:48.490
And when I think of
security, I'm like, oh, yeah,

00:07:48.490 --> 00:07:50.050
just scan my container,
and then I'll be fine.

00:07:50.050 --> 00:07:50.600
Like, whatever.

00:07:50.600 --> 00:07:53.142
But it sounds like you're being
a bit more holistic about it.

00:07:53.142 --> 00:07:53.810
Is that correct?

00:07:53.810 --> 00:07:55.602
CONNOR GILBERT: That's
a really good point.

00:07:55.602 --> 00:07:57.400
So we call it a full
lifecycle product.

00:07:57.400 --> 00:08:00.370
I mentioned earlier maybe
that they'll deploy run stages

00:08:00.370 --> 00:08:01.558
of the container lifecycle.

00:08:01.558 --> 00:08:03.100
So there are things
you want to apply

00:08:03.100 --> 00:08:05.140
in all of those stages
as your app moves

00:08:05.140 --> 00:08:07.930
toward production, but also in
the infrastructure underlying

00:08:07.930 --> 00:08:08.480
it.

00:08:08.480 --> 00:08:11.937
So when you think of making
a holistic security program,

00:08:11.937 --> 00:08:14.270
you really have to make sure
that your images are clean,

00:08:14.270 --> 00:08:15.290
make sure that they're minimal.

00:08:15.290 --> 00:08:17.710
There are a lot of things that
can have downstream effects

00:08:17.710 --> 00:08:18.740
in the build phase.

00:08:18.740 --> 00:08:20.740
In the deploy phase, there
are a ton of controls

00:08:20.740 --> 00:08:22.360
that Kubernetes exposes.

00:08:22.360 --> 00:08:24.580
A lot of people don't use
them because either they

00:08:24.580 --> 00:08:26.260
don't have any
incentive to do so,

00:08:26.260 --> 00:08:28.180
or they don't know about
it, or they find it

00:08:28.180 --> 00:08:29.510
a little bit too complicated.

00:08:29.510 --> 00:08:30.490
There's another learning curve.

00:08:30.490 --> 00:08:32.230
So once you know how
to use Kubernetes

00:08:32.230 --> 00:08:33.610
and you know how to
get your app deployed,

00:08:33.610 --> 00:08:35.860
maybe you don't want to take
another learning curve up

00:08:35.860 --> 00:08:37.580
and learn network
policies, for instance.

00:08:37.580 --> 00:08:39.622
So there's things you can
do in the deploy phase.

00:08:39.622 --> 00:08:41.690
And then in runtime,
ongoing monitoring

00:08:41.690 --> 00:08:43.780
it is important for any
kind of security threat

00:08:43.780 --> 00:08:45.260
detection or response.

00:08:45.260 --> 00:08:47.110
In addition, there's
that underlying layer

00:08:47.110 --> 00:08:50.620
of the infrastructure-- so
securing your registry, where

00:08:50.620 --> 00:08:52.270
you're storing your
container images,

00:08:52.270 --> 00:08:54.228
securing the cluster
infrastructure.

00:08:54.228 --> 00:08:56.770
There are plenty of options for
[? deploying ?] your cluster,

00:08:56.770 --> 00:09:00.640
plenty of options for managing
it, including on GKE or just

00:09:00.640 --> 00:09:01.763
on GCE.

00:09:01.763 --> 00:09:03.430
So there are a lot
of things you can do,

00:09:03.430 --> 00:09:05.350
and we try to help people
understand all of those.

00:09:05.350 --> 00:09:07.142
GABRIELA FERRARA: You
mentioned monitoring.

00:09:07.142 --> 00:09:08.800
It did get a bit
of my attention.

00:09:08.800 --> 00:09:10.780
What kind of
monitoring is StackRox

00:09:10.780 --> 00:09:13.520
offering for their
customers, for instance,

00:09:13.520 --> 00:09:20.050
if someone tried to access my
port 3306 for MySQL or a DOS

00:09:20.050 --> 00:09:20.560
attack?

00:09:20.560 --> 00:09:21.462
What it is?

00:09:21.462 --> 00:09:23.170
CONNOR GILBERT: I'll
answer by discussing

00:09:23.170 --> 00:09:26.140
a little bit about how
people run containers,

00:09:26.140 --> 00:09:28.030
and microservices especially.

00:09:28.030 --> 00:09:31.480
So if you add the microservices
design pattern into the mix,

00:09:31.480 --> 00:09:34.450
you're really operating your
application in a different way,

00:09:34.450 --> 00:09:36.100
and you're building
in a different way.

00:09:36.100 --> 00:09:40.290
So typically, people will
start their container journey

00:09:40.290 --> 00:09:42.520
by learning some best
practices and packaging, maybe,

00:09:42.520 --> 00:09:44.440
existing apps into containers.

00:09:44.440 --> 00:09:46.750
But once they've gotten
past that initial stage,

00:09:46.750 --> 00:09:49.275
they'll start building their
app to run well in a container.

00:09:49.275 --> 00:09:50.650
So there will only
be one process

00:09:50.650 --> 00:09:52.320
running, for the most part.

00:09:52.320 --> 00:09:55.570
There won't be supervisors
starting new processes every so

00:09:55.570 --> 00:09:56.140
often.

00:09:56.140 --> 00:09:58.640
Your app will have one
job, for the most part.

00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:00.310
So we monitor a
lot of those things

00:10:00.310 --> 00:10:02.185
and then say, well,
because it's a container,

00:10:02.185 --> 00:10:03.977
there are a lot of
things that you wouldn't

00:10:03.977 --> 00:10:05.080
expect to normally happen.

00:10:05.080 --> 00:10:06.760
If you're monitoring
a VM, you'll often

00:10:06.760 --> 00:10:08.712
see people installing packages.

00:10:08.712 --> 00:10:10.420
There's some system,
then, getting inside

00:10:10.420 --> 00:10:12.370
to make sure that
something is deployed

00:10:12.370 --> 00:10:13.470
or operating correctly.

00:10:13.470 --> 00:10:15.220
With containers, you
often don't see that.

00:10:15.220 --> 00:10:17.050
So you can monitor
the signals that

00:10:17.050 --> 00:10:19.730
might have been too noisy
in other infrastructures.

00:10:19.730 --> 00:10:21.760
So you can monitor those,
and then each report

00:10:21.760 --> 00:10:23.030
has more meaning.

00:10:23.030 --> 00:10:25.420
So what we do is in our
product specifically,

00:10:25.420 --> 00:10:28.180
we monitor the container
activity, including processes,

00:10:28.180 --> 00:10:29.860
network activity, other things.

00:10:29.860 --> 00:10:31.900
They take on a different
meaning in containers

00:10:31.900 --> 00:10:33.950
just because of the way the
apps are built and deployed.

00:10:33.950 --> 00:10:35.390
MARK MANDEL: So now
I'm kind of curious.

00:10:35.390 --> 00:10:37.473
You've obviously gone deep
on the container space,

00:10:37.473 --> 00:10:39.120
but you have a
security background.

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:41.720
Has containerization made
your life easier or harder

00:10:41.720 --> 00:10:43.220
from a security point of view?

00:10:43.220 --> 00:10:44.845
CONNOR GILBERT: That's
a good question.

00:10:44.845 --> 00:10:46.400
I'm in a different
position, having

00:10:46.400 --> 00:10:49.250
gotten into the container
space a little earlier

00:10:49.250 --> 00:10:50.420
than some others.

00:10:50.420 --> 00:10:51.860
Certainly not the earliest.

00:10:51.860 --> 00:10:55.860
I found out about Docker
after it was 1.5 or so.

00:10:55.860 --> 00:10:57.110
But I do have that background.

00:10:57.110 --> 00:10:58.777
And within the company,
we've got people

00:10:58.777 --> 00:11:00.560
from ops and security.

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:03.733
I think once people understand
the concepts of Kubernetes,

00:11:03.733 --> 00:11:05.900
they find that it helps
them to do their job better.

00:11:05.900 --> 00:11:10.418
So often any sort of reticence
is around unfamiliarity.

00:11:10.418 --> 00:11:11.960
It's true that there
are applications

00:11:11.960 --> 00:11:13.700
that aren't well
suited to containers,

00:11:13.700 --> 00:11:15.660
but a lot of applications are.

00:11:15.660 --> 00:11:17.960
And once you adopt the
containerization strategy,

00:11:17.960 --> 00:11:19.880
and especially
adopting Kubernetes,

00:11:19.880 --> 00:11:22.550
you can really improve your
visibility, your ability

00:11:22.550 --> 00:11:24.740
to really understand
what a deployment is,

00:11:24.740 --> 00:11:26.030
and to monitor it.

00:11:26.030 --> 00:11:27.530
Some of those
advantages are just

00:11:27.530 --> 00:11:29.580
having all the
metadata in one place.

00:11:29.580 --> 00:11:33.770
So I just spoke at BSides
San Francisco few weeks ago,

00:11:33.770 --> 00:11:36.260
and just showed on
the screen an example,

00:11:36.260 --> 00:11:38.150
actually simplified
deployment YAML,

00:11:38.150 --> 00:11:40.460
so a deployment
specification for Kubernetes.

00:11:40.460 --> 00:11:43.700
Then it's got the exact
reference to what image

00:11:43.700 --> 00:11:45.838
will run, so the exact
file system and code

00:11:45.838 --> 00:11:46.880
that's going to be there.

00:11:46.880 --> 00:11:49.088
You can have network security
policies attached right

00:11:49.088 --> 00:11:49.820
alongside.

00:11:49.820 --> 00:11:52.910
You've got the privilege
level, Linux capabilities,

00:11:52.910 --> 00:11:54.470
the user that it
should run under,

00:11:54.470 --> 00:11:56.150
the storage that
you're going to access.

00:11:56.150 --> 00:11:57.180
There's a ton of stuff.

00:11:57.180 --> 00:11:59.690
There's even annotations,
sort of an unsung hero.

00:11:59.690 --> 00:12:01.640
If your operations team
is using annotations

00:12:01.640 --> 00:12:03.210
to understand what
they're deploying,

00:12:03.210 --> 00:12:05.070
now you can see them
in security too.

00:12:05.070 --> 00:12:07.868
So really, having the Kubernetes
API lets the different teams,

00:12:07.868 --> 00:12:10.160
even if they haven't totally
learned all the Kubernetes

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:13.700
concepts, it does sort of gather
a lot of information together

00:12:13.700 --> 00:12:15.750
and help people speak
the same language.

00:12:15.750 --> 00:12:18.440
We talk about the Kubernetes
API being the language

00:12:18.440 --> 00:12:20.060
that everyone can
speak to understand

00:12:20.060 --> 00:12:21.310
what's going on in deployment.

00:12:21.310 --> 00:12:24.250
GABRIELA FERRARA: Containers,
in the end, are popular now,

00:12:24.250 --> 00:12:26.600
and everybody wants to run
their stuff in Kubernetes

00:12:26.600 --> 00:12:29.490
because it's the cool
kid on the block.

00:12:29.490 --> 00:12:32.090
What questions do
you ask your clients

00:12:32.090 --> 00:12:34.660
when they come to you
with an architecture

00:12:34.660 --> 00:12:36.930
and want to deploy
on Kubernetes?

00:12:36.930 --> 00:12:38.360
What do you ask them?

00:12:38.360 --> 00:12:39.860
CONNOR GILBERT: It
depends on really

00:12:39.860 --> 00:12:41.870
what your application does.

00:12:41.870 --> 00:12:44.540
Some applications will
work perfectly fine

00:12:44.540 --> 00:12:46.040
in a managed service.

00:12:46.040 --> 00:12:49.220
Some will work fine even in
a surrealist context, a cloud

00:12:49.220 --> 00:12:51.410
function or something like that.

00:12:51.410 --> 00:12:54.740
We find that a lot of things
that people would run on VMs

00:12:54.740 --> 00:12:56.510
can naturally move
to containers.

00:12:56.510 --> 00:12:58.760
There are even use cases
where you can use containers

00:12:58.760 --> 00:13:01.370
as a packaging strategy, but
maybe not even orchestration,

00:13:01.370 --> 00:13:04.100
so some customers will end
up running containers on VMs

00:13:04.100 --> 00:13:04.970
directly.

00:13:04.970 --> 00:13:06.050
Those are exceptions.

00:13:06.050 --> 00:13:08.760
I think most people can use
containers in Kubernetes,

00:13:08.760 --> 00:13:10.530
and they will drive
benefit from it.

00:13:10.530 --> 00:13:13.680
It's more the edge cases that
would require some extra care.

00:13:13.680 --> 00:13:15.180
GABRIELA FERRARA:
Do a lot of people

00:13:15.180 --> 00:13:17.500
want to run everything on
Kubernetes-- for instance,

00:13:17.500 --> 00:13:20.390
databases, stuff
that is stateful?

00:13:20.390 --> 00:13:21.570
Do you get that a lot?

00:13:21.570 --> 00:13:23.960
And how do you explain
to them that maybe it's

00:13:23.960 --> 00:13:25.380
not a case for that?

00:13:25.380 --> 00:13:27.047
CONNOR GILBERT: Yeah,
so I think there's

00:13:27.047 --> 00:13:29.300
been an interesting shift
in the marketplace recently.

00:13:29.300 --> 00:13:31.160
So we used to have
a lot of people come

00:13:31.160 --> 00:13:33.650
and say, oh, you guys
know containers, right?

00:13:33.650 --> 00:13:34.610
I need containers.

00:13:34.610 --> 00:13:35.860
Can you tell me what they are?

00:13:35.860 --> 00:13:36.950
Or, I need Kubernetes.

00:13:36.950 --> 00:13:38.840
Can you please tell
me how to use it?

00:13:38.840 --> 00:13:41.120
If you project back
maybe eight or 10 years,

00:13:41.120 --> 00:13:42.120
I've heard about Hadoop.

00:13:42.120 --> 00:13:43.130
I need Hadoop.

00:13:43.130 --> 00:13:45.350
Could you please
tell me what it is?

00:13:45.350 --> 00:13:47.593
So we used to be in
that space a lot more.

00:13:47.593 --> 00:13:50.010
Now we're seeing a lot more
sophistication in the customer

00:13:50.010 --> 00:13:52.183
space, knowing what's
good and what's not.

00:13:52.183 --> 00:13:54.350
Ops teams are coming up to
speed on containerization

00:13:54.350 --> 00:13:55.292
and on Kubernetes.

00:13:55.292 --> 00:13:56.000
But you're right.

00:13:56.000 --> 00:13:58.590
You mentioned databases,
so stateful applications.

00:13:58.590 --> 00:14:00.560
It has been an evolving
story in the community.

00:14:00.560 --> 00:14:03.380
I come from more of a security
background versus an ops

00:14:03.380 --> 00:14:06.260
background, so I have less
experience directly managing

00:14:06.260 --> 00:14:07.490
databases and stuff.

00:14:07.490 --> 00:14:10.190
But it's certainly true
that people have figured out

00:14:10.190 --> 00:14:12.557
different ways of using,
say, stateful sets, or even

00:14:12.557 --> 00:14:14.390
resources outside the
cluster to help manage

00:14:14.390 --> 00:14:15.432
their stateful workloads.

00:14:15.432 --> 00:14:18.290
That's one example of something
that people might not always

00:14:18.290 --> 00:14:19.923
want to put into a
Kubernetes cluster.

00:14:19.923 --> 00:14:21.090
GABRIELA FERRARA: Thank you.

00:14:21.090 --> 00:14:22.940
MARK MANDEL: I'm curious to know
as well, from your experience

00:14:22.940 --> 00:14:25.470
and what you've been seeing,
if there was one thing that you

00:14:25.470 --> 00:14:27.080
were like, I just
wish everyone would

00:14:27.080 --> 00:14:29.810
do this either across
containers or Kubernetes

00:14:29.810 --> 00:14:32.007
from a security perspective,
what would that be?

00:14:32.007 --> 00:14:32.840
Or maybe two things.

00:14:32.840 --> 00:14:34.852
If you have more
than one, it's fine.

00:14:34.852 --> 00:14:36.560
CONNOR GILBERT: If
you look at the things

00:14:36.560 --> 00:14:39.260
that containers or Kubernetes
will really helps you do,

00:14:39.260 --> 00:14:42.870
one of the main things is
understand what's going on.

00:14:42.870 --> 00:14:46.280
So there's a lot of data
in those deployments.

00:14:46.280 --> 00:14:48.500
There's a lot of data
hiding and all the specs

00:14:48.500 --> 00:14:50.240
that you've given to Kubernetes
for how to run your app,

00:14:50.240 --> 00:14:51.948
and how to keep it
running, and all that.

00:14:51.948 --> 00:14:54.950
So if people would take
a look at those specs,

00:14:54.950 --> 00:14:57.650
look at the options they've
got, and just think about

00:14:57.650 --> 00:14:59.210
whether the defaults
work for them,

00:14:59.210 --> 00:15:00.600
that would be a huge difference.

00:15:00.600 --> 00:15:04.520
So we see a lot of people just
running with default configs.

00:15:04.520 --> 00:15:07.465
And that's fine, same
default in a lot of cases.

00:15:07.465 --> 00:15:08.840
But you can get
a lot of benefit,

00:15:08.840 --> 00:15:10.382
and you can really
leverage the value

00:15:10.382 --> 00:15:13.550
of having split up your app into
containers and microservices,

00:15:13.550 --> 00:15:16.880
if you take a look at a couple
of the options in the security

00:15:16.880 --> 00:15:18.710
context and in the
pod templates that

00:15:18.710 --> 00:15:21.590
help you reduce the privileges
that your container's running

00:15:21.590 --> 00:15:24.920
with, or reduce the risk that
would happen if something were

00:15:24.920 --> 00:15:26.930
to exploit an application
vulnerability.

00:15:26.930 --> 00:15:29.780
There's one actually, really
specific one, which is you

00:15:29.780 --> 00:15:32.730
can set any container to have
a read-only root file system.

00:15:32.730 --> 00:15:35.760
So if there is a command
ejection in your application--

00:15:35.760 --> 00:15:38.260
say, this routes one, or things
like that, or shellshock--

00:15:38.260 --> 00:15:40.760
if the file system is read-only,
a lot of things that people

00:15:40.760 --> 00:15:42.073
will try to do just won't work.

00:15:42.073 --> 00:15:44.240
And there are ways you can
do-- you can add a volume

00:15:44.240 --> 00:15:45.942
instruction in your
Docker file or mount

00:15:45.942 --> 00:15:47.900
the directory in Kubernetes--
that let you have

00:15:47.900 --> 00:15:49.190
specific paths be writeable.

00:15:49.190 --> 00:15:50.815
But the read-only
root FS can be really

00:15:50.815 --> 00:15:53.060
powerful to both enforce
good development practices

00:15:53.060 --> 00:15:55.292
and also minimize the
risk of adversaries

00:15:55.292 --> 00:15:56.750
compromising your
app and spreading

00:15:56.750 --> 00:15:57.980
through the infrastructure.

00:15:57.980 --> 00:15:59.397
GABRIELA FERRARA:
Have you noticed

00:15:59.397 --> 00:16:02.780
a shift on compromised
applications

00:16:02.780 --> 00:16:05.330
since you started with
this security initiative

00:16:05.330 --> 00:16:06.740
at StackRox?

00:16:06.740 --> 00:16:09.727
Did you notice a difference
between before and after?

00:16:09.727 --> 00:16:10.560
Give you an example.

00:16:10.560 --> 00:16:12.852
There are some applications
that are really vulnerable.

00:16:12.852 --> 00:16:16.280
Usually, you can get in through
malicious HTML, JavaScript,

00:16:16.280 --> 00:16:18.560
and sometimes gain
access to the server.

00:16:18.560 --> 00:16:20.510
You don't notice
any shift on that?

00:16:20.510 --> 00:16:22.580
CONNOR GILBERT: We've done some
research, the internet as well,

00:16:22.580 --> 00:16:24.580
where we do some [? honey ?]
[? nut ?] research.

00:16:24.580 --> 00:16:26.630
We see what people
start throwing

00:16:26.630 --> 00:16:28.240
at containerized applications.

00:16:28.240 --> 00:16:30.140
And we've found that
really it makes sense.

00:16:30.140 --> 00:16:31.970
They don't know that
it's a container,

00:16:31.970 --> 00:16:34.760
so they don't try to do anything
different to the applications.

00:16:34.760 --> 00:16:37.790
People do try to attack the
new infrastructure components.

00:16:37.790 --> 00:16:40.040
So we've seen some problems
with Kubernetes dashboards

00:16:40.040 --> 00:16:41.690
being exposed on the
internet and then

00:16:41.690 --> 00:16:42.860
being used to run code.

00:16:42.860 --> 00:16:45.680
That's a new threat,
but it can be managed.

00:16:45.680 --> 00:16:48.080
For instance, if you're in
Google Kubernetes Engine,

00:16:48.080 --> 00:16:50.330
you can just use the Workloads
UI in the Cloud Console

00:16:50.330 --> 00:16:52.740
instead of exposing
a dashboard directly.

00:16:52.740 --> 00:16:55.520
So there are things you could
do to minimize the new threats.

00:16:55.520 --> 00:16:57.522
The old threats that
have really changed are--

00:16:57.522 --> 00:16:59.480
I think command ejection
is one of the biggest.

00:16:59.480 --> 00:17:01.190
And just the ability
to detect when

00:17:01.190 --> 00:17:03.325
something like that happens
is much greater because

00:17:03.325 --> 00:17:04.700
of the structure
of the container

00:17:04.700 --> 00:17:07.050
and the app in the
Kubernetes world.

00:17:07.050 --> 00:17:08.750
You won't find a
major difference

00:17:08.750 --> 00:17:11.047
in things like storage
or cross-site scripting.

00:17:11.047 --> 00:17:12.589
You won't find a
huge difference just

00:17:12.589 --> 00:17:13.890
because it's in a container.

00:17:13.890 --> 00:17:14.700
If you've still got
the same database,

00:17:14.700 --> 00:17:17.158
or you've still got the same
app, just being in a container

00:17:17.158 --> 00:17:19.150
won't change the game for those.

00:17:19.150 --> 00:17:20.650
MARK MANDEL: That
sounds reasonable.

00:17:20.650 --> 00:17:23.869
Do you see a shift, though,
in where the risk security

00:17:23.869 --> 00:17:25.310
responsibility lies?

00:17:25.310 --> 00:17:27.800
Do you see some things moving
more to the cloud providers

00:17:27.800 --> 00:17:29.680
and less to the
users, or vice versa?

00:17:29.680 --> 00:17:31.430
CONNOR GILBERT: It's
an interesting space,

00:17:31.430 --> 00:17:34.610
because the responsibilities,
even within an organization,

00:17:34.610 --> 00:17:35.630
have shifted.

00:17:35.630 --> 00:17:37.940
So in the previous world,
especially if you're not

00:17:37.940 --> 00:17:40.070
in a cloud, you may
have a process where

00:17:40.070 --> 00:17:42.830
security owns the
internet uplink,

00:17:42.830 --> 00:17:44.883
and they own the firewall box.

00:17:44.883 --> 00:17:46.550
And if you want to
get your app exposed,

00:17:46.550 --> 00:17:47.508
you have to go to them.

00:17:47.508 --> 00:17:48.860
You have to write a ticket.

00:17:48.860 --> 00:17:51.620
Whatever you have to do,
they're in the process.

00:17:51.620 --> 00:17:54.020
And one of the big shifts
with categorization

00:17:54.020 --> 00:17:56.720
is that often, developers
and operations folks

00:17:56.720 --> 00:17:58.460
are running the entire stack.

00:17:58.460 --> 00:18:00.170
The network is software-defined.

00:18:00.170 --> 00:18:03.020
The firewall has software
defined in Kubernetes.

00:18:03.020 --> 00:18:05.840
A lot of this runs in places
that don't have natural hook

00:18:05.840 --> 00:18:07.310
points for security.

00:18:07.310 --> 00:18:09.890
Among teams, there has to
be increased collaboration.

00:18:09.890 --> 00:18:11.632
Now when you get to
the cloud provider,

00:18:11.632 --> 00:18:13.340
there are different
options for deploying

00:18:13.340 --> 00:18:15.200
Kubernetes and containers.

00:18:15.200 --> 00:18:17.990
You have managed services,
like Google Kubernetes Engine.

00:18:17.990 --> 00:18:21.020
You can also run Kubernetes
using open-source tooling

00:18:21.020 --> 00:18:23.630
on top of infrastructure
as a service providers,

00:18:23.630 --> 00:18:25.640
like UCE, AWS, Azure.

00:18:25.640 --> 00:18:27.260
You can do it on-premises.

00:18:27.260 --> 00:18:29.570
The [INAUDIBLE]
responsibility changes

00:18:29.570 --> 00:18:32.053
depending on which of those
options you've selected.

00:18:32.053 --> 00:18:33.470
With Google
Kubernetes Engine, you

00:18:33.470 --> 00:18:36.170
can have Google patch
your master infrastructure

00:18:36.170 --> 00:18:38.060
and keep that
protected while you're

00:18:38.060 --> 00:18:39.953
responsible more for
your applications.

00:18:39.953 --> 00:18:41.870
If you're running
self-managed, then you still

00:18:41.870 --> 00:18:43.490
have to manage some
infrastructure components,

00:18:43.490 --> 00:18:45.865
including the master, including
the worker node security,

00:18:45.865 --> 00:18:47.300
a little bit more directly.

00:18:47.300 --> 00:18:49.340
But in any case,
the cloud provider's

00:18:49.340 --> 00:18:52.310
never really going to know
what your workload is.

00:18:52.310 --> 00:18:55.190
So it is something that
you have to do to make sure

00:18:55.190 --> 00:18:58.100
that your images are in an
ideal state, or at least

00:18:58.100 --> 00:19:00.690
a secure enough state, that you
understand your risk posture,

00:19:00.690 --> 00:19:02.190
that you know what
parts of your app

00:19:02.190 --> 00:19:03.607
you want to pay
more attention to,

00:19:03.607 --> 00:19:05.810
and that you're
monitoring for threats.

00:19:05.810 --> 00:19:07.730
The cloud provider
can't really know enough

00:19:07.730 --> 00:19:09.470
about your application
to effectively do that.

00:19:09.470 --> 00:19:11.000
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, that
sounds interesting.

00:19:11.000 --> 00:19:12.920
Flip it around to
squishy people things.

00:19:12.920 --> 00:19:15.330
From an organizational
perspective,

00:19:15.330 --> 00:19:18.440
do you see that shift happening
in how people are organized

00:19:18.440 --> 00:19:19.790
inside a company as well?

00:19:19.790 --> 00:19:21.230
CONNOR GILBERT: We do see
some changes in the way

00:19:21.230 --> 00:19:23.630
that teams are managed and
the way that people operate

00:19:23.630 --> 00:19:25.970
together in this new
world of Kubernetes-based

00:19:25.970 --> 00:19:26.840
infrastructure.

00:19:26.840 --> 00:19:29.318
For one thing, people have
to talk to each other.

00:19:29.318 --> 00:19:30.860
There have been
times where I've been

00:19:30.860 --> 00:19:33.980
on-site with a customer, maybe
doing a proof of concept,

00:19:33.980 --> 00:19:36.230
and we've had a meeting
together with, say,

00:19:36.230 --> 00:19:38.570
the security operations center
staff and then the people

00:19:38.570 --> 00:19:39.945
running the
container deployment.

00:19:39.945 --> 00:19:40.848
And they're meeting.

00:19:40.848 --> 00:19:42.140
They're introducing each other.

00:19:42.140 --> 00:19:44.200
They're shaking hands
for the first time

00:19:44.200 --> 00:19:46.430
and learning about their
infrastructure together.

00:19:46.430 --> 00:19:49.100
Usually this is when maybe the
container infrastructure is not

00:19:49.100 --> 00:19:49.790
as mature.

00:19:49.790 --> 00:19:50.990
It's a smaller deployment.

00:19:50.990 --> 00:19:53.602
But the people do have to work
together to understand this.

00:19:53.602 --> 00:19:55.310
And if they've got an
existing tool that,

00:19:55.310 --> 00:19:56.810
say, monitors their
VMs, maybe they

00:19:56.810 --> 00:19:58.940
do need to figure
out a new strategy.

00:19:58.940 --> 00:20:01.070
Whether that means
purchasing a new product,

00:20:01.070 --> 00:20:02.930
operating a new
open-source component,

00:20:02.930 --> 00:20:05.240
or just understanding where
the infrastructure is,

00:20:05.240 --> 00:20:07.080
they do have to
collaborate tighter.

00:20:07.080 --> 00:20:09.230
Now, in general, there are
trends around this, too.

00:20:09.230 --> 00:20:11.930
So just like DevOps,
there's DevSecOps.

00:20:11.930 --> 00:20:14.480
And there are some companies
that have really adopted this.

00:20:14.480 --> 00:20:16.640
It can be difficult,
because asking people

00:20:16.640 --> 00:20:18.290
to be good at one
thing is hard enough.

00:20:18.290 --> 00:20:20.270
To be an expert in three
different disciplines

00:20:20.270 --> 00:20:22.370
can be even more
difficult. But the people

00:20:22.370 --> 00:20:24.740
that really can do that
are extremely powerful.

00:20:24.740 --> 00:20:27.140
And sometimes we do see
DevOps owning the security

00:20:27.140 --> 00:20:28.360
of their application.

00:20:28.360 --> 00:20:30.020
And that can be a
really powerful match

00:20:30.020 --> 00:20:31.490
because they know
the infrastructure.

00:20:31.490 --> 00:20:33.240
They know something
about the application,

00:20:33.240 --> 00:20:35.013
and they understand
the security posture,

00:20:35.013 --> 00:20:36.680
especially if they've
got a product that

00:20:36.680 --> 00:20:37.938
can help them understand it.

00:20:37.938 --> 00:20:39.980
GABRIELA FERRARA: So beyond
full stack developer,

00:20:39.980 --> 00:20:41.853
now we have full
stack DevOps too.

00:20:41.853 --> 00:20:43.270
CONNOR GILBERT:
Yeah, there's full

00:20:43.270 --> 00:20:44.870
stack infrastructure, too, now.

00:20:44.870 --> 00:20:46.720
And you can debate
where the lines are,

00:20:46.720 --> 00:20:49.450
but if you're going
literally all the way down,

00:20:49.450 --> 00:20:52.000
some people are deploying
kernel monitoring.

00:20:52.000 --> 00:20:53.590
They're understanding
Linux kernel.

00:20:53.590 --> 00:20:56.140
And they're debugging
API responses

00:20:56.140 --> 00:20:58.780
between back end and front end
and some distributed tracing.

00:20:58.780 --> 00:21:00.430
The stack just keeps going.

00:21:00.430 --> 00:21:01.930
GABRIELA FERRARA:
What do people get

00:21:01.930 --> 00:21:03.490
wrong about containers
and Kubernetes

00:21:03.490 --> 00:21:05.440
in general that you
think they would get it

00:21:05.440 --> 00:21:07.793
right, concepts and
that kind of stuff?

00:21:07.793 --> 00:21:09.460
CONNOR GILBERT: I
think some people feel

00:21:09.460 --> 00:21:10.690
like containers
and Kubernetes will

00:21:10.690 --> 00:21:12.880
solve all of their problems
with their application

00:21:12.880 --> 00:21:13.600
infrastructure.

00:21:13.600 --> 00:21:15.790
And while they're
powerful tools,

00:21:15.790 --> 00:21:18.220
you can't just
turn on Kubernetes

00:21:18.220 --> 00:21:20.680
and then have all your
problems be solved.

00:21:20.680 --> 00:21:22.240
It's sort of like
the Go programming

00:21:22.240 --> 00:21:25.000
language is a bit opinionated
about how things ought to work.

00:21:25.000 --> 00:21:27.250
Containers and Kubernetes
are a little bit opinionated

00:21:27.250 --> 00:21:30.760
just in what they make easy, so
they make the preferred answer

00:21:30.760 --> 00:21:31.910
a bit easier.

00:21:31.910 --> 00:21:34.700
So it could be really easy
to run a stateless app.

00:21:34.700 --> 00:21:38.200
It can be really easy to get
some services that are, maybe,

00:21:38.200 --> 00:21:41.183
auto scaling or that
are easily replicatable.

00:21:41.183 --> 00:21:42.850
It can be really easy
to get that going.

00:21:42.850 --> 00:21:44.683
Some of the workloads
you mentioned earlier,

00:21:44.683 --> 00:21:46.880
like stateful workloads,
it can be a little harder.

00:21:46.880 --> 00:21:48.580
You don't get that
necessarily for free.

00:21:48.580 --> 00:21:51.130
Overall, you may get a
benefit from centralizing

00:21:51.130 --> 00:21:52.660
the management and
really learning

00:21:52.660 --> 00:21:55.920
how to keep things going in
Kubernetes for those workloads,

00:21:55.920 --> 00:21:59.350
but it isn't just a free,
sort of, we'll take our app,

00:21:59.350 --> 00:22:01.330
we'll put it in Kubernetes,
and now we're good.

00:22:01.330 --> 00:22:03.180
One of the other things
is around security.

00:22:03.180 --> 00:22:06.340
So the name of this
technology, containers,

00:22:06.340 --> 00:22:08.230
has two, maybe three,
four, or five meanings

00:22:08.230 --> 00:22:09.760
depending on who you ask.

00:22:09.760 --> 00:22:12.340
It's been really great for the
nautical stock photo industry.

00:22:12.340 --> 00:22:14.645
[FOG HORN SOUND EFFECT]

00:22:14.645 --> 00:22:16.030
[LAUGHTER]

00:22:16.030 --> 00:22:18.780
So you've got Docker
with a ship or a whale,

00:22:18.780 --> 00:22:21.590
shipping containers, a bunch
of other players in the space.

00:22:21.590 --> 00:22:23.235
I talked to
[? Varun ?] from Istio,

00:22:23.235 --> 00:22:25.860
and he said he spent a long time
with a Greek dictionary trying

00:22:25.860 --> 00:22:28.530
to come up with a Greek word
related to ships and came up

00:22:28.530 --> 00:22:30.330
with "istío" for sail.

00:22:30.330 --> 00:22:32.430
So security also
doesn't come for free.

00:22:32.430 --> 00:22:34.200
Containers are still
just processes.

00:22:34.200 --> 00:22:37.020
They have some isolation,
but you're sharing a kernel.

00:22:37.020 --> 00:22:38.790
And there is some
more surface there

00:22:38.790 --> 00:22:40.530
where it's just not
quite the same threat

00:22:40.530 --> 00:22:42.090
model that people are used to.

00:22:42.090 --> 00:22:44.577
It's not necessarily true
that VMs are more secure.

00:22:44.577 --> 00:22:45.660
They also use hypervisors.

00:22:45.660 --> 00:22:47.050
They also have problems.

00:22:47.050 --> 00:22:49.620
It's important to not just
think of these as little VMs.

00:22:49.620 --> 00:22:50.910
It's not the same thing.

00:22:50.910 --> 00:22:52.452
MARK MANDEL: That
sounds really good.

00:22:52.452 --> 00:22:55.410
So if someone's looking to
improve their understanding

00:22:55.410 --> 00:22:57.180
of security and
containers in Kubernetes,

00:22:57.180 --> 00:22:59.160
are there any particular
resources or places

00:22:59.160 --> 00:23:00.368
