00:24:53.250 --> 00:24:55.920
How do I migrate my traditional
data warehouse platform

00:24:55.920 --> 00:24:56.628
to BigQuery?

00:24:56.628 --> 00:24:57.420
Maybe it's on-prem.

00:24:57.420 --> 00:24:58.560
Maybe it's on another cloud.

00:24:58.560 --> 00:24:59.977
I'm guessing I
have lots of stuff.

00:24:59.977 --> 00:25:00.870
How do I do this?

00:25:00.870 --> 00:25:02.370
GABRIELA FERRARA:
So every migration

00:25:02.370 --> 00:25:04.650
experience that we
have [? story ?]

00:25:04.650 --> 00:25:06.970
involves three common steps.

00:25:06.970 --> 00:25:08.820
First, you have
your data migration,

00:25:08.820 --> 00:25:12.830
then your schema migration, and
then your workload migration.

00:25:12.830 --> 00:25:16.770
The data migration is the
data that's on your warehouse.

00:25:16.770 --> 00:25:19.530
Schema migration, it is
the changes that you do,

00:25:19.530 --> 00:25:23.070
the outline of your database
on the data warehouse.

00:25:23.070 --> 00:25:26.520
And workload, it's how you're
going to keep feeding that

00:25:26.520 --> 00:25:28.260
to your data warehouse.

00:25:28.260 --> 00:25:31.200
Google Cloud has built and
launched data warehouse

00:25:31.200 --> 00:25:34.465
migration service to automate
migrating data and schema

00:25:34.465 --> 00:25:38.460
to BigQuery and significantly
reduce their migration time.

00:25:38.460 --> 00:25:40.860
Because before, you had
to do everything by hand.

00:25:40.860 --> 00:25:45.000
And now we have a team that you
can consult, see how it works,

00:25:45.000 --> 00:25:48.115
get credit, and try it
out to see how it works.

00:25:48.115 --> 00:25:48.990
MARK MANDEL: Awesome.

00:25:48.990 --> 00:25:50.700
GABRIELA FERRARA: So you
have now an automater, too.

00:25:50.700 --> 00:25:51.840
